\textbf{(3) Variance: Law of Total Variance}
\$$2mm]

\[
\mathrm{Var}(e_t)
\overset{\mathrm{LTV}}{=}
\mathbb{E}\!\left[
\underbrace{
\mathrm{Var}(e_t\mid y_{1:t-1})
}_{=\,Q_t}
\right]
+
\mathrm{Var}\!\left[
\underbrace{
\mathbb{E}(e_t\mid y_{1:t-1})
}_{=\,0}
\right]
=
\mathbb{E}[Q_t]
+
\mathrm{Var}(0)
=
\mathbb{E}[Q_t].
$$

Why is
$\mathrm{Var}(e_t\mid y_{1:t-1})=Q_t$?

$$
\mathrm{Var}(e_t\mid y_{1:t-1})
=
\mathrm{Var}(Y_t-f_t\mid y_{1:t-1})
=
\mathrm{Var}(Y_t\mid y_{1:t-1})
=
Q_t.
$$

Here $f_t=\mathbb{E}(Y_t\mid y_{1:t-1})$ has already been determined
once $y_{1:t-1}$ is given, so it acts as a constant inside the
conditional variance.
\\
