\textbf{(4) Standardise}
\$$2mm]

From the Gaussian predictive distribution,
\[
\boxed{
Y_t\mid y_{1:t-1}
\sim
\mathcal N(f_t,Q_t)
}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{
e_t=Y_t-f_t
\;\Bigm|\;
y_{1:t-1}
\sim
\mathcal N(0,Q_t)
}.
$$

Since
$$
\mu=0,
\qquad
\sigma^2=Q_t,
\qquad
\sigma=\sqrt{Q_t},
$$
the usual Gaussian standardisation gives
$$
\boxed{
e_t\mid y_{1:t-1}
\sim
\mathcal N(0,Q_t)
}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{
\frac{e_t-\mu}{\sigma}
=
\frac{e_t}{\sqrt{Q_t}}
\;\Bigm|\;
y_{1:t-1}
\sim
\mathcal N(0,1)
}.
$$

Hence,
$$
\boxed{
\tilde e_t
=
\frac{e_t}{\sqrt{Q_t}}
\;\Bigm|\;
y_{1:t-1}
\sim
\mathcal N(0,1)
}.
$$
\\
