\textbf{(5) Marginalise}
\$$2mm]

The key observation is that the right-hand side has
\textbf{no $y_{1:t-1}$ in it}.

For example, imagine different possible past histories:

\[
\boxed{
y_{1:t-1}=\text{history A}
}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{
\tilde e_t\sim\mathcal N(0,1)
}
$$

$$
\boxed{
y_{1:t-1}=\text{history B}
}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{
\tilde e_t\sim\mathcal N(0,1)
}
$$

$$
\boxed{
y_{1:t-1}=\text{history C}
}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{
\tilde e_t\sim\mathcal N(0,1)
}
$$

Thus,
$$
\boxed{
\tilde e_t\mid y_{1:t-1}
\sim
\mathcal N(0,1)
\quad
\text{for every possible }y_{1:t-1}.
}
$$

Marginalising over the past,
$$
p(\tilde e_t)
=
\int
p(\tilde e_t\mid y_{1:t-1})
p(y_{1:t-1})
\,dy_{1:t-1}.
$$

Since
$$
p(\tilde e_t\mid y_{1:t-1})
=
\phi(\tilde e_t),
$$
where $\phi$ is the $\mathcal N(0,1)$ density and does not depend on
$y_{1:t-1}$,

$$
p(\tilde e_t)
=
\phi(\tilde e_t)
\underbrace{
\int
p(y_{1:t-1})\,dy_{1:t-1}
}_{=\,1}
=
\phi(\tilde e_t).
$$

Therefore,
$$
\boxed{
\tilde e_t\sim\mathcal N(0,1).
}
$$
\\
