\textbf{2. Likelihood $L$}

Note: \textcolor{red}{log} always helps the PC, but only in columns 1 and 3 helps simplification for closed form.
&
Likelihood: given data, how likely is this parameter?

$$
\begin{aligned}
L(P\mid y_{1:n,0:T})
&=p(y_{1:n,1:T}\mid y_{1:n,0},P)
\\[1mm]
&\underbrace{=}_{\text{M1a}}
\prod_{k=1}^{n}
P\bigl(Y_{k,1:T}=y_{k,1:T}\mid y_{k,0},P\bigr)
\\[1mm]
&\underbrace{=}_{\text{chain rule in time}}
\prod_{k=1}^{n}\prod_{t=1}^{T}
P\bigl(Y_{k,t}=y_{k,t}\mid y_{k,0:t-1},P\bigr)
\\[1mm]
&\underbrace{=}_{\text{Markov ppt}}
\prod_{k=1}^{n}\prod_{t=1}^{T}
P\bigl(Y_{k,t}=y_{k,t}\mid y_{k,t-1},P\bigr)
\\[1mm]
&\underbrace{=}_{\text{rewrite}}
\prod_{k=1}^{n}\prod_{t=1}^{T}
p_{y_{k,t-1},\,y_{k,t}}
\\[1mm]
&\underbrace{=}_{\text{1. M1b + 2. hom time}}
\boxed{
\prod_{i=1}^{M}\prod_{j=1}^{M}
p_{ij}^{\,n_{ij}}
}.
\end{aligned}
$$

where $n_{ij}$ is the number of transitions from $i$ to $j$.

Taking logs,
$$
\boxed{
\ell(P)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}
n_{ij}\log p_{ij}
}.
$$

\textcolor{purple}{\textbf{Intuition:} $L$ is maximized by allocating relatively high transition probability to transitions with large observed counts $n_{ij}$.}

