\textbf{3. Maximization}
&
Each row of $P$ is a separate probability vector.

Each row maximization problem is independent: if you maximize transition probability out of row $i$, it has nothing to do with row $j$.

For row $i$, maximize
$$
\sum_j n_{ij}\log p_{ij}
\qquad\text{subject to}\qquad
\sum_jp_{ij}=1.
$$

The Lagrangian is
$$
\mathcal L_i
=
\sum_jn_{ij}\log p_{ij}
-
\lambda_i
\left(
\sum_jp_{ij}-1
\right).
$$

Differentiate:
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal L_i}{\partial p_{ij}}
=
\frac{n_{ij}}{p_{ij}}-\lambda_i
=
0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
p_{ij}
=
\frac{n_{ij}}{\lambda_i}.
$$

Using
$$
1
=
\sum_jp_{ij}
=
\frac{1}{\lambda_i}\sum_jn_{ij}
=
\frac{n_{i,+}}{\lambda_i}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\lambda_i=n_{i,+}.
$$

Therefore MLE:
$$
\boxed{
\hat p_{ij}
=
\frac{n_{ij}}{n_{i,+}}
}.
$$

\emph{Interpretation:}
$$
\boxed{
\hat p_{ij}
=
\frac{\text{number of observed }i\to j\text{ transitions}}
{\text{number of observed departures from }i}
}.
$$

