\textbf{Filtering --- current state from data up to $t$:}

They are all the same formula really:
$$
\boxed{
p(S_t\mid y_{1:t})
=
\int
p(S_t\mid y_{1:t},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:t})\,d\phi
}.
$$

\textbf{Whole latent path / joint smoothing --- all hidden states using all data:}
$$
\boxed{
\pi(S_{0:T}\mid y_{1:T})
=
\int
\pi(S_{0:T}\mid y_{1:T},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:T})\,d\phi
}.
$$

\textbf{Prediction --- future hidden state:}
$$
\boxed{
p(S_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t})
=
\int
p(S_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:t})\,d\phi
}.
$$

\textbf{Prediction --- future observation:}
$$
\boxed{
p(Y_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t})
=
\int
p(Y_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:t})\,d\phi
}.
$$

&
\textbf{Filtering --- current state from data up to $t$:}
$$
\boxed{
\pi(\theta_t\mid y_{1:t})
=
\int
\pi(\theta_t\mid y_{1:t},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:t})\,d\phi
}.
$$

\textbf{Whole latent path / joint smoothing --- entire continuous path using all data:}
$$
\boxed{
\pi(\theta_{0:T}\mid y_{1:T})
=
\int
\pi(\theta_{0:T}\mid y_{1:T},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:T})\,d\phi
}.
$$

\textbf{Prediction --- future state or observation:}
$$
\boxed{
\pi(\theta_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t})
=
\int
\pi(\theta_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:t})\,d\phi
}
$$
or
$$
\boxed{
p(Y_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t})
=
\int
p(Y_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:t})\,d\phi
}.
$$
\\
\hline

\textbf{4. Plug-in vs full Bayes}
&
\textbf{Compare:}
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Plug-in frequentist:}
&
p(Y_{t+1}=j\mid Y_t=i,\hat P)=\hat p_{ij},
\\[2mm]
\text{Full Bayesian:}
&
\displaystyle
p(Y_{t+1}=j\mid Y_t=i,y)
=
\int p_{ij}\pi(P\mid y)\,dP.
\end{array}
}
$$

The Bayesian expression propagates uncertainty about $P$ rather than treating the estimated transition matrix $\hat P$ as exact.

&
\textbf{Compare:}
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Plug-in frequentist:}
&
p(Y_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t},\hat\phi),
\\[2mm]
\text{Full Bayesian:}
&
\displaystyle
p(Y_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t})
=
\int
p(Y_{t+h}\mid y_{1:t},\phi)
\pi(\phi\mid y_{1:t})\,d\phi.
\end{array}
}
$$

The Bayesian expression propagates uncertainty about $\phi$ rather than treating the estimated HMM parameters $\hat\phi$ as exact.

&
Compare:
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Plug-in frequentist:}
&
\mathcal N\bigl(f_t(\hat\phi),Q_t(\hat\phi)\bigr),
\\[2mm]
\text{Full Bayesian:}
&
\displaystyle
\int
\mathcal N\bigl(f_t(\phi),Q_t(\phi)\bigr)
p(\phi\mid y_{1:t-1})\,d\phi.
\end{array}
}
$$

The Bayesian expression propagates uncertainty about $\phi$ rather than treating one estimated value as exact.
\\
\hline

\textbf{5. Computation: MCMC and Monte Carlo}
&
\textbf{Usually easy here.}

Closed form exists.

If desired, simply draw many
$$
P^{(1)},P^{(2)},\dots\sim\pi(P\mid y)
$$
and average the predictions obtained from them.

&
\textbf{Problem:} the posterior and the integrals above may be too difficult to calculate exactly.

\textbf{MCMC intuition:} instead of calculating the whole posterior analytically, generate many plausible posterior draws
$$
(\phi^{(1)},S^{(1)}),
(\phi^{(2)},S^{(2)}),
\dots
$$

A common MCMC method is \textbf{Gibbs sampling}:
$$
\boxed{
\text{sample states given parameters}
\;\longleftrightarrow\;
\text{sample parameters given states}.
}
$$

After many iterations, these behave as draws from the desired joint posterior.

\textbf{Monte Carlo intuition:} once the draws exist, replace difficult posterior integrals by averages over the draws.
$$
\boxed{
\text{MCMC/Gibbs gets the draws;}
\qquad
\text{Monte Carlo uses/averages the draws.}
}
$$

&
same as col 2
\\
\hline
\end{tabular}

