\textcolor{orange}{\textbf{The likelihood (prediction-error decomposition).}} \textcolor{orange}{By the product rule,}
$$ \omark{L(\phi) \;=\; f(y_{1:T} \mid \phi) \;=\; \prod_{t=1}^{T} f(y_t \mid y_{1:t-1}, \phi)}. $$
\textcolor{orange}{For a DLM each factor is Gaussian, with the one-step-ahead forecast moments $(f_t, Q_t)$ delivered by the Kalman filter run at $\phi$ (DLMwR eqs. 4.1--4.2):}
$$ \omark{L(\phi) \;=\; \prod_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{N}\big(y_t;\, f_t(\phi),\, Q_t(\phi)\big), \qquad \ell(\phi) \;=\; -\tfrac12 \sum_{t=1}^{T} \log\abs{Q_t} \;-\; \tfrac12 \sum_{t=1}^{T} (y_t - f_t)' Q_t^{-1} (y_t - f_t)}. $$

Two approaches
$\bullet$ \emph{Frequentist}: maximise the likelihood over $\phi$ to get the MLE $\hat\phi$. \textcolor{orange}{Concretely $\hat\phi = \arg\max_\phi \ell(\phi)$ by numerical optimization (e.g. BFGS).} A note on identifiability: two parameter values $\phi_1 \neq \phi_2$ may give the SAME likelihood for every possible dataset. Then the $\arg\max$ is not unique --- the model is \emph{not identifiable}. Prefer the more parsimonious model.

\textcolor{orange}{\emph{The plug-in procedure and its caveat (exam June 2026 Q7a; DLMwR pp. 143, 148).} In the classical framework, after computing the MLE $\hat\phi$ one plugs it into the model and applies the Chapter-2 filtering / smoothing / forecasting recursions as if $\phi$ were a known constant.} \textcolor{orange}{This common practice suffers from the difficulty of taking properly into account the uncertainty about $\phi$: every output treats $\hat\phi$ as exact, so the resulting predictive intervals understate the total uncertainty --- they are too narrow.}

$\bullet$ \emph{Bayesian inference for $\phi$.} \textcolor{orange}{Treat $\phi$ as random with a prior $\pi(\phi)$; the model assumptions (Markov states, conditional independence of the $Y_t$ given the states) hold \emph{conditionally on} $\phi$.} \textcolor{orange}{Marginally --- with $\phi$ integrated out --- the classic properties are lost: the state process is no longer Markov and the observations are no longer conditionally i.i.d., because averaging over $\pi(\phi)$ ties all time points together.} \textcolor{orange}{Every ingredient --- $F_t, G_t, V_t, W_t$ and the prior of $\theta_0$ --- may depend on $\phi$.} \textcolor{orange}{Posterior:}
$$ \omark{f(\phi \mid y_{1:T}) \;\propto\; f(y_{1:T} \mid \phi)\, f(\phi)}, $$
\textcolor{orange}{generally not analytically manageable outside conjugate special cases $\Rightarrow$ MCMC: Gibbs sampling on the augmented $(\theta_{0:T}, \phi)$, alternating the full conditionals $\pi(\theta_{0:T} \mid \phi, y_{1:T})$ (drawn by FFBS) and $\pi(\phi \mid \theta_{0:T}, y_{1:T})$.} \textcolor{orange}{\emph{The joint density of the data (exam June 2024 Q7).} Frequentist: $p(y_{1:n}; \phi) = \prod_t p(y_t \mid y_{1:t-1}; \phi)$ (DLMwR eq. 4.1) \emph{does} depend on the unknown $\phi$.} \textcolor{orange}{Bayesian: $\phi$ is integrated out against its prior, giving the \emph{prior predictive} (evidence)}
